1
00:00:01,358 --> 00:00:07,357
CHAPTER 6 Pig and Pepper For a minute or two she stood looking at

2
00:00:07,358 --> 00:00:12,759
the house, and wondering what to do next, when suddenly a foot man in livery came

3
00:00:12,760 --> 00:00:16,959
running out of the wood—( —(she considered him to be a foot man because he was in

4
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:20,857
livery: otherwise, judging by his face only, she would have called him a fish)—

5
00:00:20,858 --> 00:00:26,439
)—and wrapped loudly at the door with his knuckles. It was opened by another

6
00:00:26,440 --> 00:00:32,797
footman in livery, with a round face, and large eyes like a frog;

7
00:00:32,798 --> 00:00:41,078
and both footmen, Alice noticed, had powdered hair that curled all over their heads.

8
00:00:41,079 --> 00:00:46,118
She felt very curious to know what it was all about, and crept a little way out

9
00:00:46,119 --> 00:00:48,279
of the wood to listen.

10
00:00:48,280 --> 00:00:53,499
The fish foot man began by producing from under his arm a great letter, nearly

11
00:00:53,500 --> 00:01:00,698
as large as himself, and this he handed over to the other, saying, in a solemn tone,

12
00:01:00,699 --> 00:01:07,238
“For the Duchess. An invitation from the Queen to play croquet.”

13
00:01:07,239 --> 00:01:11,979
The Frog footman repeated, in the same solemn tone, only changing the order of

14
00:01:11,980 --> 00:01:19,058
the words a little, “From the Queen. An invitation for the Duchess to play croquet

15
00:01:19,059 --> 00:01:23,857
they both bowed low, and their curls got entangled together.

16
00:01:23,858 --> 00:01:28,178
Alice laughed so much at this, that she had to run back into the wood for fear

17
00:01:28,179 --> 00:01:32,618
of their hearing her; and when she next peeped out the fish

18
00:01:32,619 --> 00:01:36,939
footman was gone, and the other one was sitting on the ground near the door,

19
00:01:36,940 --> 00:01:40,297
staring stupidly up into the sky.

20
00:01:40,298 --> 00:01:44,018
Alice went timidly up to the door, and knocked.

21
00:01:44,019 --> 00:01:50,558
“There’s no sort of use in knocking,” said the foot man and that for two reasons.

22
00:01:50,559 --> 00:01:56,138
First, because I’m on the same side of the door as you are; secondly, because

23
00:01:56,139 --> 00:02:01,178
they’re making such a noise inside, no one could possibly hear you.”

24
00:02:01,179 --> 00:02:06,178
And certainly there was a most extraordinary noise going on within

25
00:02:06,179 --> 00:02:12,939
constant howling and sneezing, and every now and then a great crash, as if a dish

26
00:02:12,940 --> 00:02:15,518
or kettle had been broken to pieces.

27
00:02:15,519 --> 00:02:22,779
“Please, then,” said Alice, “how am I to get in?” “There might be some sense in your

28
00:02:22,780 --> 00:02:27,337
knocking,” the footmen went on without attending to her, “if we had the door

29
00:02:27,338 --> 00:02:33,218
between us. For instance, if you were inside you might knock, and I could let

30
00:02:33,219 --> 00:02:37,658
you out, you know.” He was looking up into the sky all the

31
00:02:37,659 --> 00:02:43,297
time he was speaking, and this Alice thought decidedly uncivil.

32
00:02:43,298 --> 00:02:48,938
“But perhaps he can’t help it,” she said to herself; “his eyes are so very nearly at

33
00:02:48,939 --> 00:02:50,499
the top of his head.

34
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:58,118
But at any rate he might answer questions.— .—How am I to get in?” she repeated, aloud.

35
00:02:58,119 --> 00:03:05,737
“I shall sit here,” the Footman remarked, “till tomorrow—” At this moment the door of the house

36
00:03:05,738 --> 00:03:12,038
opened, and a large plate came skimming out, straight at the Footman’s head: it

37
00:03:12,039 --> 00:03:17,379
just grazed his nose, and broke to pieces against one of the trees behind him. “—or

38
00:03:17,380 --> 00:03:24,218
next day, maybe,” the footmen continued in the same tone, exactly as if nothing had happened.

39
00:03:24,219 --> 00:03:27,459
“How am I to get in?” asked Alice again, in

40
00:03:27,460 --> 00:03:32,919
a louder tone. Are you to get in at all?” said the Footman. “That’s the first

41
00:03:32,920 --> 00:03:39,398
question, you know.” It was, no doubt: only Alice did not like to be told so.

42
00:03:39,399 --> 00:03:44,197
“It’s really dreadful,” she muttered to herself, “the way all the creatures argue.

43
00:03:44,198 --> 00:03:49,959
It’s enough to drive one crazy!” The Footman seemed to think this a good

44
00:03:49,960 --> 00:03:53,379
opportunity for repeating his remark, with variations.

45
00:03:53,380 --> 00:03:57,578
“I shall sit here,” he said, “on and off, for

46
00:03:57,579 --> 00:04:04,658
days and days.” “But what am I to do?” said Alice. “Anything you like,” said the Footman,

47
00:04:04,659 --> 00:04:07,429
and began whistling.

48
00:04:07,430 --> 00:04:11,559
[Music]

49
00:04:11,560 --> 00:04:17,078
“Oh, there’s no use in talking to him,” said Alice desperately: “he’s perfectly idiotic!”

50
00:04:17,079 --> 00:04:22,599
And she opened the door and went in. The door led right into a large kitchen,

51
00:04:22,600 --> 00:04:28,059
which was full of smoke from one end to the other: the Duchess was sitting on a

52
00:04:28,060 --> 00:04:33,038
three-legged stool in the middle, nursing a baby; the cook was leaning over the

53
00:04:33,039 --> 00:04:37,778
fire, stirring a large cauldron which seemed to be full of soup. “There’s

54
00:04:37,779 --> 00:04:42,759
certainly too much pepper in that soup!” Alice said to herself, as well as she

55
00:04:42,760 --> 00:04:47,499
could for sneezing. There was certainly too much of it in the air. Even the

56
00:04:47,500 --> 00:04:52,657
Duchess sneezed occasionally; and as for the baby, it was sneezing and howling

57
00:04:52,658 --> 00:04:55,599
alternately without a moment’s pause.

58
00:04:55,600 --> 00:05:00,759
The only things in the kitchen that did not sneeze, were the cook, and a large cat

59
00:05:00,760 --> 00:05:05,318
which was sitting on the hearth and grinning from ear to ear.

60
00:05:05,319 --> 00:05:10,898
“Please would you tell me,” said Alice, a little timidly, for she was not quite

61
00:05:10,899 --> 00:05:18,398
sure whether it was good manners for her to speak first, “why your cat grins like that?”

62
00:05:18,399 --> 00:05:23,499
“It’s a Cheshire cat,” said the Duchess, “and that’s why.

63
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:30,578
Pig!” She said the last word with such sudden violence that Alice quite jumped; but she

64
00:05:30,579 --> 00:05:35,858
saw in another moment that it was addressed to the baby, and not to her, so

65
00:05:35,859 --> 00:05:40,478
she took courage, and went on again:— “I didn’t know that Cheshire cats always

66
00:05:40,479 --> 00:05:46,178
grinned; in fact, I didn’t know that Cheshire cats could grin.” “They all can,”

67
00:05:46,179 --> 00:05:52,538
said the Duchess; “and most of them do.” “I don’t know of any that do,” Alice said

68
00:05:52,539 --> 00:05:57,879
very politely, feeling quite pleased to have gotten into a conversation. “You

69
00:05:57,880 --> 00:06:04,118
don’t know much,” said the Duchess; “and that’s a fact.” Alice did not at all like

70
00:06:04,119 --> 00:06:08,978
the tone of this remark, and thought it would be as well to introduce some other

71
00:06:08,979 --> 00:06:14,139
subject of conversation. While she was trying to fix on one, the cook took the

72
00:06:14,140 --> 00:06:18,697
cauldron of soup off the fire, and at once set to work throwing everything

73
00:06:18,698 --> 00:06:24,278
within her reach at the Duchess and the baby— —the fire iron came first; then

74
00:06:24,279 --> 00:06:29,799
followed a shower of saucepans, plates, and dishes. The Duchess took no notice of

75
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:34,778
them even when they hit her; and the baby was howling so much already, that it was

76
00:06:34,779 --> 00:06:41,499
quite impossible to say whether the blows heard it or not. “Oh, please mind what you’re doing!”

77
00:06:41,500 --> 00:06:48,278
cried Alice, jumping up and down in an agony of terror. “Oh, there goes his precious nose

78
00:06:48,279 --> 00:06:54,697
an unusually large saucepan flew close by it, and very nearly carried it off. “If

79
00:06:54,698 --> 00:07:00,938
everybody minded their own business,” the Duchess said in a horse growl, “the

80
00:07:00,939 --> 00:07:06,879
world would go round a deal faster than it does.” “Which would not be an advantage,” said

81
00:07:06,880 --> 00:07:11,197
Alice, who felt very glad to get an opportunity of showing off a little of

82
00:07:11,198 --> 00:07:16,059
her knowledge. “Just think of what work it would make with the day and night!

83
00:07:16,060 --> 00:07:20,678
You see the earth takes 24 hours to turn round on its axis—”

84
00:07:20,679 --> 00:07:28,178
“Talking of axes,” said the Duchess, “chop off her head!” Alice glanced rather anxiously at the

85
00:07:28,179 --> 00:07:32,559
cook, to see if she meant to take the hint; but the cook was busily stirring

86
00:07:32,560 --> 00:07:40,478
the soup, and seemed not to be listening, so she went on again 24 hours, I think

87
00:07:40,479 --> 00:07:48,099
or is it 12 I—” “Oh, don’t bother me said the Duchess; “I never could abide figures!”

88
00:07:48,100 --> 00:07:53,139
And with that she began nursing her child again, singing a sort of lullaby to

89
00:07:53,140 --> 00:07:57,938
it as she did so, and giving it a violent shake at the end of every line:

90
00:07:57,939 --> 00:08:03,938
“Speak roughly to your little boy, And beat him when he sneezes: He only does it

91
00:08:03,939 --> 00:08:10,618
to annoy, Because he knows it teases.” CHORUS. (In which the cook and the baby joined):

92
00:08:10,619 --> 00:08:18,999
“Wow! wow! wow!” While the Duchess sang the second verse of the song, she kept tossing the baby

93
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:23,559
violently up and down, and the poor little thing howled so, that Alice could

94
00:08:23,560 --> 00:08:29,978
hardly hear the words:— “I speak severely to my boy, I beat him when he sneezes; For

95
00:08:29,979 --> 00:08:38,618
he can thoroughly enjoy The pepper when he pleases Wow! wow! wow!”

96
00:08:38,619 --> 00:08:44,019
“Here! you may nurse it a bit, if you like!” the Duchess said to Alice, flinging the

97
00:08:44,020 --> 00:08:50,318
baby at her as she spoke. “I must go and get ready to play croquet with the Queen,”

98
00:08:50,319 --> 00:08:52,358
and she hurried out of the room.

99
00:08:52,359 --> 00:08:56,858
The cook threw a frying pan after her as she went out, but it just missed her.

100
00:08:56,859 --> 00:09:00,639
Alice caught the baby with some difficulty, as it was a queer-shaped

101
00:09:00,640 --> 00:09:05,559
little creature, and held out its arms and legs in all directions, “just like a

102
00:09:05,560 --> 00:09:08,259
starfish thought Alice.

103
00:09:08,260 --> 00:09:12,578
The poor little thing was snorting like a steam engine when she caught it, and

104
00:09:12,579 --> 00:09:16,419
kept doubling itself up and straightening itself out again, so that

105
00:09:16,420 --> 00:09:20,919
all together for the first minute or two, it was as much as she could do to hold

106
00:09:20,920 --> 00:09:24,759
it. As soon as she had made out the proper way of nursing it, (which was to

107
00:09:24,760 --> 00:09:26,678
twist it up into a sort of knot, and then

108
00:09:26,679 --> 00:09:32,379
keep tight hold of its rear ear and left foot, so as to prevent its undoing itself,)

109
00:09:32,380 --> 00:09:35,197
she carried it out into the open air.

110
00:09:35,198 --> 00:09:37,898
“If I don’t take this child away with me,”

111
00:09:37,899 --> 00:09:41,858
thought Alice, “they’re sure to kill it in a day or two:

112
00:09:41,859 --> 00:09:47,259
wouldn’t it be murder to leave it behind?” She said the last words out loud, and the

113
00:09:47,260 --> 00:09:52,118
little thing grunted in reply (it had left off sneezing by this time). “Don’t

114
00:09:52,119 --> 00:09:57,578
grunt,” said Alice; “that’s not at all a proper way of expressing yourself.”

115
00:09:57,579 --> 00:10:03,398
The baby grunted again, and Alice looked very anxiously into its face to see what

116
00:10:03,399 --> 00:10:05,139
was the matter with it.

117
00:10:05,140 --> 00:10:09,759
There could be no doubt that it had a very turnip nose, much more like a snout

118
00:10:09,760 --> 00:10:15,697
than a real nose; also its eyes were getting extremely small for a baby:

119
00:10:15,698 --> 00:10:19,538
altogether Alice did not like the look of the thing at all.

120
00:10:19,539 --> 00:10:24,278
“But perhaps it was only sobbing,” she thought, and looked into its eyes again,

121
00:10:24,279 --> 00:10:26,919
to see if there were any tears.

122
00:10:26,920 --> 00:10:29,499
No, there were no tears.

123
00:10:29,500 --> 00:10:34,118
“If you’re going to turn into a pig, my dear,” said Alice, seriously, “I’ll have

124
00:10:34,119 --> 00:10:40,478
nothing more to do with you. Mind now!” The poor little thing sobbed again (or

125
00:10:40,479 --> 00:10:46,778
grunted, it was impossible to say which), and they went on for some while in silence.

126
00:10:46,779 --> 00:10:51,697
Alice was just beginning to think to herself, “Now, what am I to do with this creature

127
00:10:51,698 --> 00:10:55,898
when I get it home?” when it grunted again, so violently, that

128
00:10:55,899 --> 00:10:59,318
she looked down into its face in some alarm.

129
00:10:59,319 --> 00:11:05,438
This time there could be no mistake about it: it was neither more nor less than a pig,

130
00:11:05,439 --> 00:11:10,237
and she felt that it would be quite absurd for her to carry it further.

131
00:11:10,238 --> 00:11:14,799
So she left a little creature down, and felt quite relieved to see it trot away

132
00:11:14,800 --> 00:11:17,078
quietly into the wood.

133
00:11:17,079 --> 00:11:23,019
“If it had grown up,” she said to herself, “it would have made a dreadfully ugly child:

134
00:11:23,020 --> 00:11:28,419
but it makes rather a handsome pig, I think.” And she began thinking over other

135
00:11:28,420 --> 00:11:34,118
children she knew, who might do very well as pigs, and was just saying to herself,

136
00:11:34,119 --> 00:11:39,697
“if one only knew the right way to change them—” when she was a little startled by

137
00:11:39,698 --> 00:11:44,139
seeing the Cheshire Cat sitting on a bow of a tree a few yards off.

138
00:11:44,140 --> 00:11:47,398
The Cat only grinned when it saw Alice.

139
00:11:47,399 --> 00:11:55,778
It looked good-natured, she thought: still it had very long claws and a great many

140
00:11:55,779 --> 00:12:01,078
teeth, so she felt that it ought to be treated with respect

141
00:12:01,079 --> 00:12:07,178
she began, rather timidly, as she did not at all know whether it would like the name:

142
00:12:07,179 --> 00:12:13,657
however, it only grinned a little wider comets pleased so far,” thought Alice, and

143
00:12:13,658 --> 00:12:19,059
she went on. “Would you tell me, please, which way I ought to go from here?”

144
00:12:19,060 --> 00:12:25,419
“That depends a good deal on where you want to get to,” said the Cat.

145
00:12:25,420 --> 00:12:32,019
“I don’t much care where—” said Alice. “Then it doesn’t matter which way you go,” said

146
00:12:32,020 --> 00:12:33,038
the Cat.

147
00:12:33,039 --> 00:12:40,657
“—so long as I get somewhere Alice added as an explanation. “Oh, you’re sure to do that,”

148
00:12:40,658 --> 00:12:46,178
said the Cat, “if you only walk long enough.” Alice felt that this could not be

149
00:12:46,179 --> 00:12:48,697
denied, so she tried another question.

150
00:12:48,698 --> 00:12:53,438
“What sort of people live about here?” “In that direction,”

151
00:12:53,439 --> 00:12:58,118
the Cat said, waving its right paw around lives a Hatter:

152
00:12:58,119 --> 00:13:03,818
and in that direction,” waving the other paw, “lives in March hair

153
00:13:03,819 --> 00:13:10,178
Visit either you like: they’re both mad.” “But I don’t want to go among mad people,”

154
00:13:10,179 --> 00:13:12,578
Alice remarked.

155
00:13:12,579 --> 00:13:19,178
“Oh, you can’t help that,” said the Cat: “we’re all mad here.

156
00:13:19,179 --> 00:13:25,718
I’m mad. You’re mad.” “How do you know I’m mad?” said Alice. “You

157
00:13:25,719 --> 00:13:29,799
must be,” said the Cat, “or you wouldn’t have come here.”

158
00:13:29,800 --> 00:13:34,657
Alice didn’t think that proved it at all; however, she went on

159
00:13:34,658 --> 00:13:38,618
“And how do you know that you’re mad?” “To begin with,”

160
00:13:38,619 --> 00:13:43,599
said the Cat, “a dog’s not mad. You grant that?”

161
00:13:43,600 --> 00:13:49,299
“I suppose so,” said Alice. “Well, then,” the Cat went on,

162
00:13:49,300 --> 00:13:52,959
“you see, a dog growls when it’s angry, and

163
00:13:52,960 --> 00:13:58,778
wags its tail when it’s pleased. Now I growl when I’m pleased, and wag my tail

164
00:13:58,779 --> 00:14:07,419
when I’m angry. Therefore I’m mad.” I call it purring, not growling,” said

165
00:14:07,420 --> 00:14:10,358
Alice. “Call it what you like,” said the Cat.

166
00:14:10,359 --> 00:14:15,278
“Do you play croquet with the Queen today I should like it very much,” said Alice,

167
00:14:15,279 --> 00:14:22,839
“but I haven’t been invited yet.” “You’ll see me there,” said the Cat, and vanished.

168
00:14:22,840 --> 00:14:29,019
Alice was not much surprised at this, she was getting so used to queer things happening.

169
00:14:29,020 --> 00:14:35,499
While she was looking at the place where it had been, it suddenly appeared again by the by

170
00:14:35,500 --> 00:14:41,799
what became of the baby?” said the Cat. “I’d nearly forgotten to ask.”

171
00:14:41,800 --> 00:14:47,379
“It turned into a pig,” Alice quietly said, just as if it had

172
00:14:47,380 --> 00:14:50,019
come back in a natural way.

173
00:14:50,020 --> 00:14:54,999
“I thought it would,” said the Cat, and vanished again.

174
00:14:55,000 --> 00:15:00,459
Alice waited a little, half expecting to see it again, but it did not appear,

175
00:15:00,460 --> 00:15:04,839
and after a minute or two she walked on in the direction in which the March hair

176
00:15:04,840 --> 00:15:06,578
was said to live.

177
00:15:06,579 --> 00:15:11,799
“I’ve seen hatters before,” she said to herself; “the March hair will be much the

178
00:15:11,800 --> 00:15:16,178
more interesting, and perhaps as this is May it won’t be

179
00:15:16,179 --> 00:15:21,999
raving mad— —at least not so mad as it wasn't March.” As she said this, she looked

180
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:26,978
up, and there was the Cat again, sitting on a branch of a tree.

181
00:15:26,979 --> 00:15:31,959
“Did you say pig, or fig?” said the Cat.

182
00:15:31,960 --> 00:15:37,299
“I said pig reply to Alice; “and I wish you wouldn’t keep appearing and vanishing so

183
00:15:37,300 --> 00:15:43,898
suddenly: you make one quite giddy.” “All right,” said the Cat; and this time it

184
00:15:43,899 --> 00:15:49,358
vanished quite slowly, beginning with the end of the tale and ending with the grin,

185
00:15:49,359 --> 00:15:53,737
which remained sometime after the rest of it had gone.

186
00:15:53,738 --> 00:15:59,499
“Well! I’ve often seen a cat without a grin,” thought Alice; “but a grin without a

187
00:15:59,500 --> 00:16:03,818
cat! It’s the most curious thing I ever saw in my life!”

188
00:16:03,819 --> 00:16:09,398
She had not gone much farther before she came in sight of the house of the March hair

189
00:16:09,399 --> 00:16:12,879
she thought it must be the right house, because the chimneys were shaped like

190
00:16:12,880 --> 00:16:16,178
ears and the roof was thatched with fur.

191
00:16:16,179 --> 00:16:20,678
It was so large a house, that she did not like to go nearer till she had nibbled

192
00:16:20,679 --> 00:16:24,759
some more of the left-handed bit of mushroom, and raised herself to about two

193
00:16:24,760 --> 00:16:29,919
feet high: even then she walked up towards it rather timidly, saying to

194
00:16:29,920 --> 00:16:34,059
herself “Suppose it should be raving mad after all!

195
00:16:34,060 --> 00:16:37,000
I almost wish I’d gone to see the Hatter instead
